{{Nihongo|Kazuki Hino|日野 一樹|Hino Kazuki|born May 15, 1980}} is a [[Sanese people|Sanese]] ([[Okaiken]]) retired professional [[w:bodybuilder|bodybuilder]], gym owner, and coach, widely regarded as the greatest heavyweight competitor in the history of the sport. He is best known for his record-tying eight-year reign as '''[[Mr. Adonia]]''' (2007–2015), the most prestigious title in professional bodybuilding, from which he retired undefeated.

Nicknamed "{{nihongo|'''The Iron Monk'''|鉄の僧|Tetsu no sō}}" and "{{nihongo|'''The Saitama Beast'''|埼玉の獣|Saitama no kemono}}", Hino is celebrated for his immense size, "grainy" conditioning, and the monastic discipline that defined every aspect of his life — from his training and nutrition to his refusal to own a television or a smartphone until 2019. He is credited with popularizing the "Mass Monster" era in Okaiken and establishing the country as a powerhouse in global bodybuilding. Since his retirement, he has operated the {{nihongo|[[Iron Temple (gym)|Iron Temple]]|鉄寺|Tetsumidera}}, a gym in Saitama that has become a pilgrimage site for bodybuilders worldwide.

==Early life==

===Childhood and family===
Hino was born in [[Saitama]], [[Miyagi Prefecture]], [[Okaiken]], the elder of two sons in a working-class family. His father, {{nihongo|Daigorō Hino|日野大五郎|Hino Daigorō}}, was a steel-fabrication foreman at the [[Saitama Heavy Industries]] plant; his mother, {{nihongo|Tomoko Hino|日野知子|Hino Tomoko}}, worked as a school cafeteria supervisor. The family occupied a two-bedroom municipal housing unit in the Shimo-Fukushima district, an area of light industry and working-class residences that Hino has described as "the kind of place where men came home with metal dust in their hair and the bathwater ran grey."

Hino's relationship with his father was formative but distant. Daigorō was a man of few words who expressed care through labor — he built the family's dining table and shelving by hand from scrap steel, and Kazuki would later describe the sound of his father's arc welder through the apartment wall as "the first iron I ever heard." Daigorō never explicitly encouraged or discouraged Kazuki's bodybuilding ambitions; when Kazuki won his first Mr. Adonia title in 2007, Daigorō's only comment was reportedly, "You're still using your hands. That's good." Daigorō died of a cardiac event in 2011, during the peak of Kazuki's competitive reign. Hino competed at the 2011 Mr. Adonia three months after his father's death, dedicating his victory to "the first man who showed me that iron answers honesty."

His younger brother, {{nihongo|Junji Hino|日野淳二|Hino Junji}}, pursued a career in civil engineering and has no involvement in bodybuilding. The brothers remain close but maintain very different lives; Junji has described Kazuki as "the same person he was at fifteen — just much, much larger."

===The volleyball rejection===
[[File:Hino volleyball team 1995.png|thumb|right|250px|Hino (back row, far right) with the Saitama North High School volleyball team, 1995 — the year he was cut from the roster.]]
In his youth, Hino was described as tall but exceptionally lean — "lanky" in the words of his junior high coach. He initially pursued [[volleyball]] at Saitama North High School, drawn to the sport's vertical emphasis and the possibility that height could compensate for his lack of mass. He trained with the team for two years, but at age 15, he was cut during the annual roster reduction. The coach, {{nihongo|Takeshi Morimoto|森本剛|Morimoto Takeshi}}, reportedly told Hino that he lacked the "physical presence" to compete at the prefectural level — a phrase Hino has cited in virtually every interview since.

Morimoto's assessment was not inaccurate by the standards of competitive volleyball. Hino at 15 was 175 cm and 62 kg — a frame that, while tall for his age, lacked the explosive lower-body power and lateral quickness the sport demanded. But the rejection's impact was not in its accuracy but in its redirection. Hino has described the moment he read the posted roster and did not find his name as "the first time I understood that the world measures men by what they carry, not by how they move." He walked past the volleyball gym and, for the first time, entered the weight room at the far end of the athletic hall.

===Discovery of bodybuilding===
In 1995, Hino discovered a back issue of the Shaydian bodybuilding magazine ''Iron Republic'' at a used-book shop in Saitama Station. The cover featured [[United Midlands|Shaydian]] bodybuilder [[Rick Dixon|Rick "The Mason" Dixon]] in a most-muscular pose — a physique of dense, granite-like muscularity that bore no resemblance to the lean, athletic build volleyball had demanded. Hino has described the experience as "seeing a man who had been measured and found sufficient."

He purchased the magazine for ¥200 and began training the same week at the Saitama Municipal Gym, a concrete-block facility with rusted plate-loaded machines and a single power rack. He adopted Dixon's "blood and guts" philosophy from the magazine's training column, emphasizing heavy compound movements and extreme intensity. He taught himself Mercian by watching Dixon's training VHS tapes, which he rented from a specialty video shop in Okami's Shimbashi district, adopting the curse words as "mantras of power" — a practice that would later evolve into his famous {{nihongo|"Iron Howl"|鉄の叫び|Tetsu no sakebi}}.

Within six months, Hino had gained 14 kg. Within two years, he had outgrown the municipal gym's equipment and begun training at the [[Saitama Iron Works Gym]], a facility attached to the same industrial complex where his father worked. The gym's owner, a retired steelworker named {{nihongo|Kōsaku Iba|伊庭耕作|Iba Kōsaku}}, allowed Hino to train after hours in exchange for cleaning the facility. Iba would become Hino's first mentor, teaching him the fundamentals of progressive overload and introducing him to the {{nihongo|[[Cult of Iron]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} tradition through a worn copy of [[Murakami Motosuke]]'s ''[[Chronicles of the Iron Path]]''.

==Bodybuilding career==

===Amateur career (1998–2005)===
[[File:Hino amateur 2004.png|thumb|right|250px|Hino at the 2004 All-Okaiken National Championships, where his second-place finish behind Shōhei Takeda ignited the mass-versus-conditioning debate.]]
Hino competed in his first bodybuilding contest at age 18, the 1998 Saitama Prefectural Novice Championship, placing 6th in the heavyweight division. He was, by his own admission, "terrible" — his posing was wooden, his tan was uneven, and he wore board shorts because he did not own posing trunks. But his size was already noteworthy; at 19, he weighed 108 kg with visible abdominal separation.

He spent the next seven years climbing the amateur ranks with a single-mindedness that disturbed his family and peers. He declined university admission, worked a series of manual-labor jobs (construction, warehouse loading, scrap-metal sorting) that he chose specifically for their physical demands, and structured his entire existence around training, eating, and sleeping. He ate the same meal — rice, grilled mackerel, and miso soup — for 267 consecutive days in 2002, a period he refers to as "the year of the fish."

His amateur breakthrough came at the 2004 All-Okaiken National Championships, where he placed 2nd in the super-heavyweight division behind the veteran {{nihongo|Shōhei Takeda|武田将平|Takeda Shōhei}}. The loss was controversial; many in the audience felt Hino's size overwhelmed Takeda's superior conditioning. The debate presaged the mass-versus-conditioning split that would define his professional career.

In 2005, Hino won the '''Okaiken Pro''', his first major national title, weighing in at {{convert|128|kg|lb|abbr=on}}. The victory was decisive; judges noted that his leg development was already years ahead of the domestic standard. He earned his professional card and immediately announced his intention to compete for Mr. Adonia.

===International debut and early struggles (2006)===
[[File:Hino 2006 Grand Prix.png|thumb|left|250px|Hino at the 2006 United Midlands Grand Prix, his international debut — a humbling third-place finish that drove his "year of the wall" transformation.]]
Hino's international debut at the 2006 '''United Midlands Grand Prix''' was a humbling experience. Despite his size, he placed 3rd, losing to more aesthetically polished competitors. Critics argued that while Hino possessed "freakish" mass, he lacked the stage presence and refined posing of the top-tier western pros. The IFCB judging criteria — which weighted extreme conditioning and aesthetic proportion more heavily than the domestic {{nihongo|"living density"|生きた密度|ikita mitsudo}} standard — exposed weaknesses Hino had never been forced to address.

Hino took this criticism with a characteristic response: he disappeared. He retreated to the Saitama Iron Works Gym for what he called "the year of the wall" — twelve months of isolation training in which he refined his conditioning, learned to pose from a retired Tesshin Kyōkai judge named {{nihongo|Hiroshi Zaizen|財津浩|Zaizen Hiroshi}}, and rebuilt his stage presentation from the ground up. He did not compete again until the 2007 Mr. Adonia.

===The Adonia Dynasty (2007–2015)===

====2007–2010: The Era of Granite====
[[File:Hino Mr Adonia 2007.png|thumb|right|250px|Hino hitting his signature "Most Muscular" pose at the 2007 Mr. Adonia, his first world title — the first ever by an Okaiken competitor.]]
Hino returned in 2007 to the '''Mr. Adonia''' stage, the sport's highest honor. Weighing in at a shredded {{convert|135|kg|lb|abbr=on}}, he shocked the bodybuilding world by defeating the reigning champion [[Titus Grol]] in a landslide victory. His physique displayed a new "grainy" texture — a look of extreme muscle hardness and separation — that would become his signature. The victory was the first by an Okaiken competitor in the history of the Mr. Adonia competition.

Between 2008 and 2010, Hino established dominance, winning every title with perfect scores. This period is often referred to as "{{nihongo|The Era of Granite|御影石の時代|Mikageishi no jidai}}." His routine of hitting a "Most Muscular" pose while unleashing his {{nihongo|"Iron Howl"|鉄の叫び|Tetsu no sakebi}} — a series of guttural vocalizations that echoed through the competition venue — became a fan-favorite spectacle. Opponents who attempted to out-size him on the posedown floor found themselves standing next to a man who seemed to expand in every direction simultaneously.

The 2009 Mr. Adonia saw Hino at his heaviest competitive weight — {{convert|137|kg|lb|abbr=on}} — a figure that prompted the IFCB to formally review its weight-class definitions. The 2010 competition produced what remains the only "perfect score" in Mr. Adonia history: all nine judges placed Hino first in every round.

====2011–2012: The Wars====
[[File:Hino Endo posedown 2011.png|thumb|left|300px|The Hino–Endo posedown at the 2011 Mr. Adonia — the most controversial decision in the competition's history, decided 5-4 in Hino's favor.]]
The middle of Hino's reign was defined by two concurrent rivalries that together produced the most sustained period of competitive debate in the sport's modern history.

On the international stage, his rivalry with United Midlands bodybuilder '''Matt Mulhouse''' — the "aesthetic" antithesis to Hino's "mass monster" style — produced the era's most visible media spectacle. Mulhouse, a champion of the Western bodybuilding tradition with mathematically precise proportions and ballet-like posing, represented everything the IFCB judging criteria were designed to reward. The rivalry reached its peak at the 2012 Mr. Adonia, where Hino allegedly engaged in a physical intimidation tactic backstage, slamming his shoulder into Mulhouse before the final posedown. The incident, witnessed by several competitors and officials, was not formally sanctioned but became one of the most discussed moments in the competition's history.

Simultaneously, the domestic rivalry with fellow Sanese competitor '''[[Satoshi Endo|Satoshi "The Granite" Endo]]''' — whose extreme conditioning represented the philosophical counterpoint to Hino's mass within the Okaiken judging tradition — was the closer competitive threat. Endo placed as runner-up to Hino at both the 2011 and 2012 Mr. Adonia, ahead of Mulhouse on the scorecards both years. The 2011 decision was particularly controversial: the judges' scorecards were split 5-4, with the deciding factor being the {{nihongo|Peak & Peak|ピーク・アンド・ピーク}} round, in which Hino's volcanic intensity narrowly edged Endo's granite stillness. The three-way tension between mass, conditioning, and aesthetic proportion dominated the 2011–2012 competitive season and generated unprecedented media coverage of bodybuilding in Okaiken.

====2013: The torn pec====
[[File:Hino torn pec 2013.png|thumb|right|250px|Hino at the 2013 Mr. Adonia, visibly asymmetrical from a torn right pectoral — he won by a single point.]]
In 2013, Hino faced his toughest challenge after suffering a partial tear of his right pectoralis major three weeks before the Mr. Adonia competition. The injury occurred during a heavy incline barbell press — a movement Hino considered the cornerstone of his chest development — when he felt what he described as "a cloth tearing inside my chest" on the fourth repetition at 175 kg.

Medical staff advised withdrawal. Hino refused. He modified his training to protect the injury, eliminated all direct chest work, and focused his final three weeks on leg and back development — areas where he already held a decisive advantage over the field. On competition day, the right side of his chest was visibly flatter than the left, and he adjusted his posing to minimize the asymmetry, emphasizing front-lat-spread and rear-double-biceps poses that de-emphasized the pectoral. He won by a single point — the narrowest victory margin of his career — and dedicated the title to his father, who had died earlier that year.

====2014–2015: The final forge====
[[File:Hino Mr Adonia 2015.png|thumb|left|250px|Hino's final Mr. Adonia victory in 2015 — his eighth consecutive title, after which he retired undefeated.]]
He returned in 2014 fully healed and larger than ever, delivering what many historians consider the greatest package of his career — {{convert|136|kg|lb|abbr=on}} with full muscle bellies, grainy conditioning, and a back spread that ''Tesshin'' magazine described as "a cobra hood made of granite."

Hino announced his retirement immediately after winning his eighth title in 2015, removing his posing trunks and placing them on the center stage podium. He retired undefeated as Mr. Adonia, stating he wished to focus on raising his sons, [[Renji Hino]] and [[Raijin Hino]], and on the gym that had become his life's work. In his acceptance speech, he said: "The iron does not retire. The iron waits. I will answer it in a different room now."

