====Rivalries====
{{main|Hino–Endo rivalry|Hino–Mulhouse rivalry}}
Hino's competitive career was defined by two concurrent rivalries that dominated the 2011–2012 seasons:

* '''Hino vs. Endo''': The domestic debate between mass and conditioning, mapped directly onto the Tesshin Kyōkai's divergence from IFCB judging philosophy. Endo represented the {{nihongo|"Second Forge"|第二の鍛冶|Daini no kaji}} — the mind's mastery over the body through extreme discipline. Hino represented the {{nihongo|"First Forge"|第一の鍛冶|Dai'ichi no kaji}} — raw, overwhelming mass. The rivalry was decided 5-4 in 2011 and 7-2 in 2012, with mass prevailing both times.
* '''Hino vs. Mulhouse''': The international narrative between mass and aesthetic proportion. Mulhouse's golden-ratio proportions and ballet-like posing provided the philosophical counterpoint to Hino's density. Mulhouse never placed above 2nd, but the rivalry generated unprecedented international media coverage and the infamous backstage incident at the 2012 Mr. Adonia.

===Training style ("The Iron War")===
[[File:Hino training Iron Temple.png|thumb|right|250px|Hino training at the Iron Temple, 2019. The gym's bare concrete walls and single mirror remain unchanged since its founding.]]
{{main|Iron War (training system)}}
Hino is the originator of a high-volume, high-intensity training system he calls {{nihongo|"'''The Iron War'''"|鉄の戦争|Tetsu no sensō}} or {{nihongo|"'''Total Annihilation'''"|全滅|Zenmetsu}}. The system has been adopted as the standard curriculum for competitive programs at [[Iron Body Center]]s under the [[National Iron Body Initiative]].

====Philosophy====
The Iron War system is grounded in the {{nihongo|[[Three Forges]]|三つの鍛冶|Mittsu no Kaji}} doctrine of [[Murakami Motosuke]], which posits that the body, mind, and spirit must be tempered simultaneously through physical discipline. Hino's specific interpretation emphasizes the following principles:

* {{nihongo|''Kakugo''|覚悟|"Acceptance of consequence"}}: The psychological practice of entering each training session with complete willingness to suffer. Hino has described this as "the moment before the first rep when you accept that this will hurt, and you choose it anyway."
* The {{nihongo|"Iron Howl"|鉄の叫び|Tetsu no sakebi}}: Guttural vocalization during maximal efforts, framed within the Three Forges philosophy as an expression of the Second Forge (mental tempering). Hino's howls — which have been described as ranging from "a samurai's kiai" to "something between a war cry and an orgasm" — are explicitly taught rather than discouraged, in contrast to the silence norms of many Western gymnasium cultures.
* Intra-set posing: The practice of hitting competitive poses between sets to direct blood into target muscles and develop the neuromuscular connection between training and presentation. Hino would hit a most-muscular pose between every set of every exercise, a practice that added an estimated 40 minutes to his daily training time.
* The {{nihongo|"three great roots"|大根三節|Daikon sansetsu}}: The barbell squat, the deadlift, and the incline barbell press — the three compound movements Hino considers the structural foundation of all physique development.

====A typical training day====
Hino's training schedule during his competitive career followed an unvarying pattern that he maintained for over a decade:

He woke at 4:30 AM without an alarm — a habit he developed at age 16 and has never broken. He ate his first meal (rice, grilled fish, miso) in silence, reviewing his training log from the previous session. He arrived at the gym by 5:45 AM and spent 15 minutes in the {{nihongo|shrine alcove|神棚|kamidana}} at the gym's entrance, performing the {{nihongo|''shugyō''|修行}} rites of the Cult of Iron tradition — a brief meditation and hand-clapping ritual that he has described as "checking in with the iron."

Training began at 6:00 AM sharp. A typical chest session during the 2011 season, as documented by ''Tesshin'' magazine in a 2012 profile, consisted of:

# Incline barbell press: Warm-up sets to 180 kg, then 3 working sets of 6–8 reps, each set followed by a most-muscular pose held for 10 seconds.
# Flat dumbbell press: 4 sets of 8–10 reps with a 3-second pause at the bottom of each rep.
# Weighted dips: 4 sets to failure, with additional weight added until failure occurred between 8–12 reps.
# Cable crossovers: 3 sets of 15 reps, performed with extreme focus on the stretch position and a 5-second hold at peak contraction.
# Dumbbell pullovers: 3 sets of 12 reps, performed across a bench to expand the ribcage and fascia.

The session lasted approximately 90 minutes. Hino did not rest between exercises — he moved immediately from one to the next, using the transition time for intra-set posing. He did not listen to music. He did not check his phone (he did not own one until 2019). He did not speak to anyone unless the communication was directly related to the training.

After training, he consumed a post-workout meal prepared by his wife, Yumi, and spent 30 minutes in the gym's communal bath — the {{nihongo|''Tesshin-yu''|鉄身湯|"iron body bath"}} — before returning to his apartment on the floor above the gym. He ate six identical meals per day, weighed himself four times, and recorded every rep of every set in a leather-bound training log that he has maintained continuously since 1996. He went to bed at 9:00 PM.

This routine did not vary for holidays, birthdays, or the birth of his son. When Renji was born in 2009, Hino trained at 4:00 AM — two hours earlier than usual — so he could be present at the hospital by 7:00 AM. He returned to the gym at 11:00 AM for his second session.

====Off-season versus contest preparation====
In the off-season (approximately October through February), Hino increased his caloric intake to roughly 6,500 kcal/day and focused on progressive overload, pushing his working weights to their annual peaks. His off-season weight reached {{convert|145|kg|lb|abbr=on}}, and he trained with slightly longer rest periods, allowing heavier loads.

Contest preparation began in March and lasted approximately 16 weeks. Calories were reduced incrementally — never by more than 200 kcal per week — while training volume was maintained or increased. Hino's approach to contest preparation was notable for its refusal to adopt the extreme dehydration and sodium-manipulation protocols common among IFCB competitors; he argued that these practices violated the Three Forges principle that the body should be "hard because it is forged, not because it is drained." He consumed a minimum of 4 liters of water per day until the final 48 hours before competition, and his sodium intake was reduced but never eliminated. This approach produced his signature "grainy" conditioning — a look of dense, dry muscularity that retained fullness rather than the flat, depleted appearance that extreme protocols sometimes produced.

==The Iron Temple==

===Founding===
[[File:Iron Temple exterior.png|thumb|right|250px|The exterior of the Iron Temple in Saitama's Shimo-Fukushima industrial district, 2024. The hand-painted sign above the entrance reads {{nihongo|"Discipline. Acceptance. Iron."|修行・覚悟・金属|Shugyō. Kakugo. Kinzoku}}.]]
The {{nihongo|Iron Temple|鉄寺|Tetsumidera}} is Hino's gym in Saitama, widely regarded as the most hardcore bodybuilding facility in Okaiken and a pilgrimage site for competitive bodybuilders worldwide. The name — which Hino chose himself — reflects the gym's status as both a training facility and a site of the {{nihongo|[[Cult of Iron]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} tradition.

The gym's origins trace to Hino's amateur career. After outgrowing the Saitama Iron Works Gym, he began training in a converted warehouse space in the Shimo-Fukushima industrial district, renting a 200-square-meter unit from the same industrial complex where his father had worked. The space had no heating, no air conditioning, and a single bathroom. Hino trained there alone for three years, from 2002 to 2005, gradually adding equipment purchased from closing gyms and fabrication shops. He welded his own squat racks from scrap steel. He poured his own dumbbells. The only luxury was a second-hand mirror, salvaged from a bankrupt department store, that he mounted on the east wall.

When Hino won the Okaiken Pro in 2005, the prize money allowed him to formally lease the space and begin converting it into a proper gym. He hired no architect and no designer. The layout was determined entirely by his training needs: the power rack in the center, the dumbbell area to the left, the cable machines to the right, and the posing mirror on the east wall — the same mirror he had salvaged years earlier. He added a {{nihongo|shrine alcove|神棚|kamidana}} at the entrance, a communal bath in the back, and a small apartment on the second floor where he continues to live.

===Philosophy and culture===
The Iron Temple operates on principles that are explicitly stated on a painted sign above the entrance:

# {{nihongo|''Shugyō''|修行|Discipline}}: Training is worship. The gym is a temple. Act accordingly.
# {{nihongo|''Kakugo''|覚悟|Acceptance}}: You will suffer. Choose it.
# {{nihongo|''Kinzoku''|金属|Iron}}: The iron does not lie. It does not flatter. It does not care about your feelings. It answers only effort.

The gym has no membership fee. Hino funds it through his competition earnings, endorsement deals, and the sale of Iron Temple-branded training equipment. Access is by invitation only; prospective members must complete a single trial — a training session alongside Hino himself. Those who complete the session without quitting are admitted. Those who quit are not invited back. Hino estimates that approximately 60% of applicants quit during the trial.

The gym's culture is deliberately austere. There is no music. There are no televisions. There are no smoothie bars or juice stations. There is a single water fountain. The walls are bare concrete, and the only decoration is the shrine alcove and a framed photograph of Rick Dixon's most-muscular pose — the same image from the 1995 magazine cover that inspired Hino to begin training. The gym's temperature is not regulated; it is cold in winter and hot in summer, a condition Hino describes as "the forge does not have climate control."

The Iron Temple received an estimated 300,000 visitors in 2025, many of whom travel to Saitama specifically to train at or visit the gym. The local tourism board has described it as "Saitama's most unlikely attraction."

===The Nakayama incident (2018)===
[[File:Hino Nakayama 2018.png|thumb|left|250px|Hino (left) and [[Rento Nakayama]] (right) at the 2018 All-Okaiken National Championships, minutes before their backstage confrontation.]]
In 2018, during the All-Okaiken National Championships at the Okami National Athletic Hall, Hino — then two years retired and attending as a spectator and guest judge — was involved in a backstage confrontation with [[Rento Nakayama]], then a Men's Physique Class B competitor who would later become Minister of Health and Welfare.

According to multiple witness accounts, the incident began when Nakayama, then 25 and competing in the Men's Physique division, entered the warm-up area where Hino was observing the open-weight competitors. Nakayama, who was filming a segment for his "Ren-Fit" social media brand, reportedly began recording near the warm-up area without the consent of the competitors present. Hino, who has a well-documented hostility toward cameras in training spaces, asked Nakayama to stop recording. Nakayama reportedly refused, citing his media credentials and arguing that the warm-up area was a public space.

The confrontation escalated. Witnesses reported that Hino stepped between Nakayama and his camera, placed his hand on the lens, and said: "This is a forge. Not a studio. Turn it off." Nakayama, who is significantly smaller than Hino (approximately 178 cm / 85 kg in competition condition versus Hino's 180 cm / 145 kg off-season weight), reportedly held his ground and replied: "The forge belongs to everyone now, old man."

Hino then reportedly grabbed Nakayama by the collar of his competition jacket and pushed him against the wall, holding him there for several seconds before releasing him. No punches were thrown. Security intervened and separated the two. Nakayama competed later that evening, placing 8th in the Men's Physique Class B division.

The incident was not reported to police, and no formal complaint was filed. However, it became widely known within the Okaiken bodybuilding community and was reported in detail by ''Tesshin'' magazine in their coverage of the 2018 championship. The magazine's editorial position was sympathetic to Hino, describing the gym as "a sacred space that demands respect" and criticizing Nakayama's "self-branding impulse" as incompatible with the {{nihongo|''shugyō''|修行}} tradition.

Nakayama addressed the incident publicly only once, in a 2024 interview during his OSP leadership campaign, saying: "I was a kid with a camera. He was a legend who didn't want to be filmed. We were both right about the other and wrong about ourselves. I respect what he built. I don't respect how he treats people who weren't born in his temple."

Hino has never publicly commented on the incident beyond a single statement to ''Tesshin'' magazine in 2019: "The iron does not need an audience. The man who trains for the camera trains for the camera, not for the iron. I treated him as the iron would treat him — honestly."

The incident has acquired additional significance in retrospect due to Nakayama's subsequent political career. As the architect of the [[National Iron Body Initiative]] (NIBI), Nakayama would become the political figure most responsible for the expansion of bodybuilding's institutional infrastructure in Okaiken — including the Iron Body Centers that adopted Hino's Iron War system as their standard curriculum. The irony of the "old man" who dismissed Nakayama's vision later having his training philosophy institutionalized by the same man's political program has been noted by multiple commentators.

==Personal life==

===Marriage and family===
[[File:Hino family 2020.png|thumb|right|250px|Hino with his wife Yumi and sons Renji (left) and Raijin (right) at the Iron Temple, 2020.]]
Hino is married to '''Yumi Hino''' (née {{nihongo|Takahashi|高橋|Takahashi}}), a former precision caterer who manages his business affairs, nutrition, and schedule. They met in 2006 when she personally delivered a meal prep order he had complained about — the rice had been undercooked by what Hino described as "an unforgivable two minutes." Yumi, then a 24-year-old culinary school graduate running a one-woman meal-prep service, arrived at his gym to argue the point. According to Hino, she stood in the doorway of the gym and said, "If you want perfect rice, cook it yourself. If you want someone to cook it for you while you lift, pay me and stop complaining." He hired her the day. They married in 2006.

Yumi's role in Hino's career extends far beyond nutrition. She manages his endorsement contracts, schedules his public appearances (of which there are very few), and serves as the operational administrator of the Iron Temple. She is widely credited within the Okaiken bodybuilding community as the logistical foundation that made Hino's monastic training lifestyle possible. ''Tesshin'' magazine's 2012 profile of Hino devoted more space to Yumi's meal-prep system than to any single training session, noting that "the Iron Monk does not cook. The Iron Monk does not shop. The Iron Monk does not think about food between meals. This is possible only because Yumi Hino exists."

They have two sons: [[Renji Hino]] (born 2009) and [[Raijin Hino]] (born 2013). Hino has described fatherhood as "the only thing that has ever made me consider stopping mid-set." He has never missed one of his sons' school events, though he typically arrives directly from the gym and leaves immediately after. When Raijin was born in 2013 — the same year as the torn-pec Mr. Adonia — Hino was at the hospital for the birth at 2:00 AM and on the competition stage three weeks later. He has said that Raijin's name, derived from the thunder god, was chosen because "he arrived like a storm — loud, sudden, and I could not stop him."

[[Renji Hino]] has followed his father into competitive bodybuilding.
[[File:Renji Hino junior 2026.png|thumb|right|250px|Renji Hino at the 2026 Saitama Prefectural Junior Championship, where he placed 2nd in the super-heavyweight division at age 17.]] At 17, Renji competed in the 2026 Saitama Prefectural Junior Championship, placing 2nd in the super-heavyweight division at a bodyweight of {{convert|112|kg|lb|abbr=on}} — drawing immediate comparisons to his father's amateur career. ''Tesshin'' magazine described Renji as "the monk's apprentice — the same density at seventeen that Kazuki brought at twenty-one, but with a waistline his father never had." Hino has coached Renji personally since 2024, applying a modified Iron War protocol scaled for the younger Hino's developing frame. When asked whether he wanted Renji to surpass his records, Hino replied: "The iron does not compete with the iron. The iron continues."

Raijin, 13, has shown interest in [[Goriki]] strongman competition rather than bodybuilding, drawn to the event's emphasis on raw functional strength over aesthetic presentation. Hino has described this divergence with characteristic brevity: "Different forge. Same fire."

===Lifestyle and discipline===
[[File:Hino Iron Howl training.png|thumb|left|250px|Hino unleashing the {{nihongo|"Iron Howl"|鉄の叫び|Tetsu no sakebi}} during a heavy deadlift at the Iron Temple, 2022.]]
Hino is known for his reclusive, monastic lifestyle, rarely appearing in public outside of competitions or gym-related events. He resides in an apartment located within the same industrial building as the Iron Temple — a space he has described as "a place to lie down between sessions." The apartment contains a bed, a table, a shrine alcove, and a bookshelf. He did not own a television until 2019, when Yumi purchased one over his objection. He did not own a mobile phone until 2019, when the Iron Temple's operational needs required it. He has never maintained a social media presence.

His daily schedule has not changed substantively since 2002. He wakes at 4:30 AM. He eats, trains, eats, trains, eats, bathes, eats, sleeps. He goes to bed at 9:00 PM. He has maintained this schedule through his competitive career, his retirement, and the operation of the Iron Temple. He has never taken a vacation. When asked by a ''Tesshin'' interviewer in 2020 what he does for relaxation, Hino replied: "I train. Then I rest. Then I train again. What is relaxation?"

Despite his intimidating appearance, he is known within his local community for a strict code of honor he calls {{nihongo|"Ronin Loyalty"|浪人忠義|Rōnin chūgi}}, famously intervening to protect respectful gym members from harassment. He has been documented on multiple occasions confronting individuals who bullied or intimidated smaller or less experienced gym members, in one case physically removing a man from the Iron Temple for mocking a novice's lifting form. His code extends beyond the gym; he is known in the Shimo-Fukushima district for paying the rent of elderly neighbors during periods of economic hardship and for personally delivering meals to shut-in residents, though he has never acknowledged these acts publicly.

===Relationship with the Cult of Iron===
[[File:Hino shrine alcove Iron Temple.png|thumb|right|200px|The {{nihongo|shrine alcove|神棚|kamidana}} at the entrance of the Iron Temple, formally recognized by the local Shoto shrine as a place of worship.]]
Hino's relationship with the {{nihongo|[[Cult of Iron]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} tradition is complex. He practices the {{nihongo|''shugyō''|修行}} rites daily and has installed a shrine alcove in both the Iron Temple and his apartment. He has described the Three Forges doctrine as "the only philosophy that has ever told me the truth about what I am doing." However, he has also been critical of what he sees as the tradition's tendency toward mystification, stating in a 2018 interview: "The iron is not a god. The iron is a tool. The forge is not a temple. The forge is a room where work happens. What makes it sacred is the work, not the room."

This pragmatic interpretation has placed him at odds with more orthodox practitioners of the Cult of Iron, who view his refusal to describe the training process in spiritual terms as a diminishment of the tradition. The debate mirrors the broader tension within Okaiken bodybuilding between the spiritual and the practical — a tension that Hino himself embodies, practicing the rites while refusing to mystify them.

==Legacy and cultural impact==
[[File:Hino Iron Festival 2025.png|thumb|left|300px|The annual {{nihongo|Iron Festival|鉄祭|Tetsu matsuri}} at the Iron Temple, 2025 — thousands gather to celebrate Hino's first Mr. Adonia victory.]]

Hino is considered a cultural icon in [[Okaiken]], often compared to a '''[[Nio]]''' (guardian deity statue) due to his intimidating physique and stoic demeanor. His success led to a surge in gym memberships across the country — the so-called "Hino Effect" — and the normalization of "hardcore" training aesthetics over the traditional "soft" aesthetics of the region.

His influence extends beyond competitive bodybuilding. The Iron War training system has been adopted as the standard curriculum for [[Iron Body Center]]s under the [[National Iron Body Initiative]], making Hino's training philosophy the de facto national standard for competitive bodybuilding programs. His emphasis on the {{nihongo|"Iron Howl"|鉄の叫び|Tetsu no sakebi}} and intra-set posing has become characteristic of Okaiken gym culture, distinguishing it from the more restrained training environments of neighboring nations.

The Iron Temple, which receives an estimated 300,000 visitors annually, has become a site of cultural significance that transcends bodybuilding. The {{nihongo|shrine alcove|神棚|kamidana}} at its entrance has been formally recognized by the local [[Shoto]] shrine as a place of worship, and the annual {{nihongo|Iron Festival|鉄祭|Tetsu matsuri}} — held on the anniversary of Hino's first Mr. Adonia victory — draws participants from across Okaiken and abroad.

In 2020, Hino was the subject of a documentary film, {{nihongo|''The Iron Does Not Retire''|鉄は引退しない|Tetsu wa intai shinai}}, directed by [[Shunsuke Mitani]] of ''[[Tetsu no Hana]]'' magazine. The film, which documented a year in Hino's life at the Iron Temple, was praised for its unflinching portrayal of his discipline and criticized by some reviewers as "hagiography." It won the [[Okami Documentary Film Festival]] Grand Prize in 2021.

